 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed changes reach browsers over server-managed socket sessions rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Polling is the simplest stateless operation and meets the freshness target, but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag; a hosted service offers the quickest low-latency path with less connection work, but brings recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets keep updates fast without tying the board to a realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect/catch-up behavior. See [Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md).
